categories:
- id: 1
  title: Core Architecture & Workflow
  description: Foundational decisions about how the WebDesign ToolKit operates, including
    AI-driven development and interface strategy.
  created_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:07'
  modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:18'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: AI-Driven Front-End Infrastructure
    problem: Manual front-end development does not scale for a multi-brand toolkit
      where components, integration, and UI testing must stay autonomous and consistent
      across natural-language constraints.
    decision: Shift from manual front-end development to an AI-agent-driven workflow.
      The repository acts as a unified toolkit where LLMs handle component generation,
      integration, and UI testing based on natural language constraints.
    pros:
    - Maximizes agent autonomy
    - Unifies generation, integration, and testing in one toolkit
    - Aligns the repo with LLM-native workflows
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:18'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: TUI/CLI First Approach
    problem: Jumping straight into complex web layouts risks weak foundations for
      agent tooling and slows early iteration on the developer-facing toolkit.
    decision: Initial toolkit and development efforts will focus on Terminal User
      Interfaces (TUI) and Command Line Interfaces (CLI) before expanding into complex
      web layouts.
    pros:
    - Establishes a strong developer-friendly foundation
    - Keeps early scope focused and iterable
    - Delays complex web layout concerns until the agent toolkit is solid
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:18'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 2
  title: Design System & Component Sourcing
  description: Decisions about visual guidelines, aesthetic enforcement, and how UI
    building blocks are sourced for LLM-friendly generation.
  created_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:11'
  modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:24'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Semantic Design Guidelines (DESIGN.md)
    problem: JSON design tokens and Figma exports require complex parsers and are
      poorly aligned with how LLMs natively consume design intent.
    decision: Adopt plain-text DESIGN.md files (based on the VoltAgent/Stitch standard)
      to define visual themes, color roles, and typography, instead of relying on
      JSON design tokens or Figma exports. Place a descriptive DESIGN.md in each brand
      directory.
    pros:
    - LLMs process Markdown natively
    - Agents instantly understand look and feel without parsers
    - Keeps brand visual intent human-readable and versionable
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:24'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: Anti-Slop Enforcement (SKILL.md)
    problem: Unconstrained LLM generation tends toward generic AI aesthetics (slop),
      weak layout choices, and noisy motion unless quality preferences are enforced
      before code is written.
    decision: Integrate the Taste Skill framework using SKILL.md files to enforce
      strict aesthetic, layout, and motion rules. Keep these files version-controlled
      in the root /SKILLS/ directory.
    pros:
    - Prevents generic AI-generated UI
    - Encodes high-quality design preferences before generation
    - Centralizes reusable aesthetic rules for all agents
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:24'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 3
    title: Open-Source Component Sourcing
    problem: Heavy NPM dependency trees bloat the toolkit and give agents brittle,
      hard-to-refactor building blocks.
    decision: Prefer copy-paste, zero-dependency component libraries (Cult UI, Skiper
      UI) and MCP-enabled motion libraries (Originkit).
    pros:
    - Reduces NPM dependency bloat
    - Provides ready-to-use high-quality blocks
    - Makes LLM refactoring and adaptation easier
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:24'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 3
  title: Repository Structure & Brand Isolation
  description: Decisions about directory layout, brand IP protection, project segregation,
    and history tracking.
  created_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:12'
  modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:30'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Strict Brand Context Isolation (/BRANDS/)
    problem: Brand-specific configuration mixed into shared repository paths risks
      IP leakage and causes LLMs to hallucinate or bleed context across design systems.
    decision: All brand-specific configuration, DESIGN.md guidelines, brandbooks,
      and overviews (README.md) must be isolated inside a git-ignored /BRANDS/{brand}/
      directory.
    pros:
    - Protects intellectual property
    - Prevents cross-brand context contamination
    - Gives each brand a clear, isolated source of truth
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:30'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: Granular and Decentralized History Tracking
    problem: A single shared history log mixes toolkit architecture evolution with
      brand/client design history, making both harder to reason about.
    decision: 'Maintain split history logs: a git-ignored /history/HISTORY.md at the
      root for global architectural decisions, and a local /history/HISTORY.md inside
      each brand directory.'
    pros:
    - Separates toolkit codebase evolution from brand design evolution
    - Keeps brand history local and private
    - Improves context focus for agents and maintainers
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:30'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 3
    title: Project-Level Segregation
    problem: Multiple applications under the same brand can share source code or project-specific
      decisions unintentionally, diluting agent focus and coupling unrelated work.
    decision: Introduce a /Projects/ subdirectory within each brand folder as /BRANDS/{brand}/Projects/{project_name}/
      so applications do not share source code or project-specific historical decisions.
    pros:
    - Isolates project contexts for the LLM
    - Prevents cross-project source and history coupling
    - Supports multiple apps per brand cleanly
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:30'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 4
  title: Agent Orchestration & Documentation
  description: Decisions about LLM guidance files, language policy, and how agents
    are constrained at runtime.
  created_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:12'
  modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:38'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Agent Orchestration via AGENTS.md
    problem: Without a mandatory root guide, agents lack a single index of tools/skills
      and may cross-contaminate brand data across executions.
    decision: A mandatory AGENTS.md file must be present at the repository root to
      act as the master guide for the LLM. It indexes available tools/skills and explicitly
      forbids cross-contaminating brand data, ensuring the agent only reads one /BRANDS/{brand}/
      context per execution.
    pros:
    - Provides a single orchestration entrypoint
    - Indexes tools and skills for the agent
    - Enforces one-brand-per-execution isolation
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:38'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: English as the Primary Repository Language
    problem: Mixed-language repository structure and global docs reduce consistency
      for global contributors and weaken LLM comprehension of shared toolkit context.
    decision: The repository structure, source code, and global documentation (including
      this ADR) must be exclusively in English. User-specific and brand-specific content
      (e.g., files inside /BRANDS/ and local history/ folders) are exempt and may
      use the user's native language.
    pros:
    - Standardizes the toolkit for global usage
    - Improves LLM comprehension of shared docs and code
    - Keeps brand-local content flexible for native languages
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:38'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
- id: 5
  title: Tooling, Testing & Provisioning
  description: Decisions about UI testing strategy, forbidden tooling, and environment
    setup automation.
  created_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:12'
  modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:38'
  decisions:
  - id: 1
    title: Vision-Based Autonomous Testing (Microsoft Isolation)
    problem: DOM-based UI tests are fragile, and Microsoft-backed tooling such as
      Playwright introduces unwanted corporate dependency and telemetry concerns.
    decision: Use Midscene.js paired with Puppeteer for headless UI testing, strictly
      banning Microsoft-backed tools like Playwright. Prefer vision-based phenomenological
      checks over brittle DOM assertions.
    pros:
    - Ensures corporate independence
    - Avoids unwanted telemetry surfaces
    - Reduces test fragility via visual/phenomenological validation
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:38'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  - id: 2
    title: Automated Environment Provisioning
    problem: Installing third-party UI tools, agents, and testing frameworks manually
      leads to inconsistent environments across machines.
    decision: Include a single install.sh bash script in the repository root to standardize
      setup so all third-party UI tools, agents, and testing frameworks install seamlessly
      in the same environment.
    pros:
    - Standardizes onboarding and setup
    - Reduces environment drift
    - Installs agents and testing frameworks together
    cons: []
    modified_at: '2026-10-01T13:15:38'
    sub_decisions: []
    deprecated: false
  deprecated: false
